= Ban Xia (Pinellia, 半夏)

_Scope: this entry treats *半夏 (bànxià)*, the dried tuber of *Pinellia ternata* (Thunb.) Makino (Araceae) — the premier phlegm-transforming and vomiting-arresting drug of Chinese medicine, and *the clearest case in this book of processing as detoxification*. Raw 生半夏 is classed *有毒* (toxic) and is essentially an external-use drug: taken by mouth it produces severe burning, numbness, and swelling of the mouth and throat, with airway compromise possible. Everything used internally is a *processed form*, and the tradition maintains at least five of them — 清半夏, 姜半夏, 法半夏, 半夏曲, 竹瀝半夏 — each made with a different corrective agent and each inclining the drug differently. Where ginger's 生薑/乾薑 and rehmannia's 生/熟 pairs show processing *redirecting* a safe drug, 半夏 shows processing *making a drug usable at all*. Status: classical and ancient — in the Shennong Bencao Jing, but in the *下品* (lower, "toxic, not for prolonged use") class, which is itself a statement about it. Not classical in Ayurveda, Tibb, or Greco-Roman medicine, though all three know *other* acrid Araceae requiring the same kind of preparation, and that parallel is treated rather than invented. The classical antidote and standing companion is ginger — see ginger.typ, which carries the other half of this relationship._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin / botanical:* *_Pinellia ternata_ (Thunb.) Makino*, family *Araceae* (the arum family — taro, konjac, calla, dieffenbachia, cuckoo-pint). The drug is the *dried tuber* (塊莖), round and pale, gathered in summer or autumn. The genus commemorates *Giovanni Vincenzo Pinelli* (1535–1601), the Italian humanist and botanical patron; the epithet *_ternata_* is "in threes," for the three-lobed leaf. *Family membership is the governing fact of this entry*: Araceae characteristically bear *calcium oxalate raphides* — bundles of needle-shaped crystals in specialized cells — and the family's acrid, mouth-burning character, from taro to dieffenbachia to cuckoo-pint, traces to them. Every culture that has used an aroid for food or medicine has had to solve the same problem.#footnote[Botanical placement and the genus eponym (Giovanni Vincenzo Pinelli; the genus established by Tenore) are standard in botanical-nomenclature references — the eponym should be verified against a specific nomenclatural source. Calcium oxalate raphides as the characteristic Araceae irritant structure: standard plant-anatomy and toxicology literature; see Modern Pharmacology for their role in this species.]

*Chinese:* *半夏 (bànxià)* — literally *"half summer,"* and *the name is a calendar marker, not a description of the plant*. The classical explanation, given in the *禮記·月令 (Lǐjì, "Yuèlìng"* — the "Monthly Ordinances" chapter of the Record of Rites) and repeated throughout the Bencao tradition, is that the plant appears at the *midpoint of summer*: *五月半夏生*, "in the fifth month, ban xia grows." The drug is named for *when it comes up*. Li Shizhen records the account in the *本草綱目*.

*Older synonyms* from the *神農本草經* and early literature: *地文 (dìwén,* "earth pattern"), *守田 (shǒutián,* "field-guardian" — it is a weed of cultivated fields), *水玉 (shuǐyù,* "water jade"). *三葉半夏* names it by the three-lobed leaf.#footnote[半夏 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (神農本草經), 下品 class, with the synonyms 地文, 守田, 水玉. The 五月半夏生 phenological account: _Lǐjì_ (禮記), "Yuèlìng" (月令) chapter; cited and discussed by Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578, 半夏 entry. The name-as-calendar-marker reading is the standard classical explanation rather than a modern folk etymology, though like all such accounts it should be read as the tradition's own rationalization of an older name.]

*Japanese:* *半夏 (hange)* the drug — *and the plant names a day in the Japanese calendar.* *半夏生 (hangeshō)* is one of the *雑節 (zassetsu)*, the supplementary seasonal markers of the traditional Japanese agricultural year: roughly 1–2 July, the eleventh day after the summer solstice, named for the emergence of this plant. It carried a hard agricultural deadline — *rice transplanting had to be finished by hangeshō* — and regional customs attach to it, including the Kansai practice of eating octopus on that day. *A medicinal weed that gives its name to a calendar date and a farming deadline is an unusual kind of cultural footprint*, and it descends directly from the same phenological observation that produced the Chinese name.

*Korean:* *반하 (banha)*.

*English:* *pinellia* (the usual pharmacognostic name), *crow-dipper* (a genuine English common name for the species, from the shape of the spathe), *ban xia* in clinical usage.

*Sanskrit:* *no name.* _Pinellia ternata_ is an East Asian plant with no entry in the Indian canon — see the Ayurveda section, where the *structural* parallel with Indian acrid aroids is treated.

*Arabic / Persian:* *no classical name.* See the Tibb section.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

*Status — and the grade matters.* 半夏 is in the *神農本草經*, but in the *下品 (xià pǐn,* lower class) — the third of the three grades, defined in that text as drugs that *treat disease, possess toxicity (有毒), and are not to be taken over long periods*. Most of this book's Chinese entries so far have been *上品* (superior: ginseng, poria, chrysanthemum, Job's tears, sesame). 半夏 is the first substantial *下品* drug treated here, and the classification is not an insult but a *clinical instruction*: potent, effective, time-limited.

*Property (性 xìng):* Warm (溫).

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Acrid / pungent (辛).

*Toxicity (毒性):* *有毒 (yǒu dú,* toxic) — *a formal part of the classical property line*, not a modern addendum. Chinese materia medica states toxicity as a drug attribute alongside property and flavour, and 半夏's entry has carried it from the Han canon onward.

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Lung (肺).

*Actions and indications:*
- *燥濕化痰 (zào shī huà tán)* — dries damp and transforms phlegm. *The defining action, and 半夏 is the category's chief drug*: for cough with copious white sputum easily expectorated, chest oppression, nausea, dizziness and a greasy tongue coat. The base formula is *二陳湯 (Èr Chén Tāng)*, in which 半夏 is sovereign (see `tangerine-peel.typ`, `fuling.typ`), and on the classical principle that phlegm arises from undrained damp and that the Spleen is its source (*治痰先治脾*).
- *降逆止嘔 (jiàng nì zhǐ ǒu)* — directs counterflow downward and stops vomiting. *The premier classical antiemetic.* The minimal expression is *小半夏湯 (Xiǎo Bàn Xià Tāng)* — 半夏 with 生薑, two drugs, from the *金匱要略* — which is simultaneously the formula and the safety pairing, since ginger is also the classical corrective for pinellia's toxicity.
- *消痞散結 (xiāo pǐ sàn jié)* — disperses *痞 (pǐ,* focal distention or "glomus": a subjective stuffed fullness below the heart, *without* pain on pressure) and dissipates nodules. The formula family is the *瀉心湯* group, above all *半夏瀉心湯 (Bàn Xià Xiè Xīn Tāng)*, which treats glomus arising from mixed cold and heat by combining acrid-warm openers with bitter-cold descenders (see `licorice-root.typ`).
- *Throat and qi-constraint register:* *半夏厚朴湯 (Bàn Xià Hòu Pò Tāng)* is the canonical formula for *梅核氣* ("plum-pit qi," the sensation of a lump in the throat that cannot be swallowed or spat out; modern globus pharyngeus) — see `ginger.typ`, which notes 生薑's dual role there as both active ingredient and pinellia-detoxifier.
- *External use (raw drug):* 生半夏, ground and applied, for sores, swellings, and cervical nodules — *the one register in which the unprocessed drug is properly used*.

*Cautions and incompatibilities:*
- *陰虛燥咳 (yin-vacuity dry cough)* — contraindicated. The drug is warm, acrid and *drying*; a patient whose cough is dry and whose deficit is fluid will be made worse.
- *Bleeding patterns*, and heat-phlegm patterns, unless the drug is specifically processed for them (竹瀝半夏, below).
- *半夏反烏頭* — pinellia *counteracts aconite* (烏頭 / 附子), one of the canonical *十八反* pairs, in the mnemonic line *半蔞貝蘞及攻烏* (pinellia, trichosanthes, fritillaria, ampelopsis and bletilla "attack" aconite). *Flagged as genuinely contested:* a substantial body of modern and historical practice does combine 半夏 with 附子 — the combination appears in transmitted formulas and in current clinical use — and the pharmacological basis of the 十八反 prohibitions is not established. The prohibition is canonical; whether it is correct is an open question, and this book reports both.
- *Pregnancy* — traditionally cautioned, *and yet* the *金匱要略* prescribes *小半夏加茯苓湯* for vomiting in pregnancy, and pinellia-containing formulas remain in use for hyperemesis in China and Japan. The classical corpus is internally divided here; the tension is real and is not resolved by this entry.#footnote[半夏 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_, 下品 class with the 有毒 designation; Zhang Zhongjing, _Jīn Guì Yào Lüè_ (金匱要略) for 小半夏湯 and 小半夏加茯苓湯; _Shāng Hán Lùn_ (傷寒論) for the 瀉心湯 family and 小柴胡湯. 半夏反烏頭 among the 十八反: standard Bencao-and-formula literature; the contested status of the 十八反 prohibitions in modern practice and research is well documented and is flagged rather than adjudicated here. Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.]

=== The processed drug-forms — the heart of this drug

_Raw pinellia is not a usable internal medicine. Every internal preparation is a processed form, and the tradition distinguishes them by *which corrective agent* was used, each inclining the drug toward a different one of its actions. This is the most elaborate detoxification-processing system treated in this book._

- *生半夏 (shēng bànxià,* raw) — *toxic*; external application only, or internal use only under specialist control. Ingested raw it causes intense burning and numbness of the mouth, tongue and throat, salivation, swelling, hoarseness, and in severe cases airway compromise; the classical descriptions of the raw drug's effect are vivid and accurate.
- *清半夏 (qīng bànxià)* — processed with *白礬 (báifán,* alum). Inclines toward *燥濕化痰*; the general-purpose phlegm form.
- *姜半夏 (jiāng bànxià)* — processed with *ginger juice and alum*. Inclines toward *降逆止嘔*; the antiemetic form. *The choice of ginger is not incidental*: ginger is independently the classical corrective for pinellia toxicity (`ginger.typ`: 解半夏天南星毒), so the processing agent, the synergist, and the antidote are one drug.
- *法半夏 (fǎ bànxià)* — processed with *甘草 (licorice)* and *石灰 (lime)*. Gentler and more purely damp-drying; the mildest of the common forms.
- *半夏曲 (bànxià qū,* "pinellia leaven") — *fermented* with flour and accessory herbs; for phlegm complicated by *food stagnation*, bringing a digestive action the plain forms lack.
- *竹瀝半夏 (zhúlì bànxià)* — processed with *竹瀝 (zhúlì,* bamboo sap, a cold phlegm-clearing drug). *This one inverts the drug's thermal nature*: a warm, drying herb is converted into a preparation usable for *hot* phlegm — the clearest instance in this book of processing reversing rather than merely intensifying a property, comparable to the 生地/熟地 inversion in `rehmannia.typ` running the other way.

*A prescribing consequence worth stating plainly:* "半夏" alone is an incomplete specification. A prescription, a formula text, or a supplement label that does not say *which* processed form is in use has left out information that changes both the action and the safety of the drug.#footnote[Processing forms and their differing emphases: _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ (current edition), which monographs 清半夏, 姜半夏 and 法半夏 as separate preparations, and Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The toxic presentation of raw pinellia (oropharyngeal burning, numbness, oedema) is described in both the classical literature and modern toxicology — see Modern Pharmacology for the raphide mechanism.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Reasoned analysis, not classical._ Pinellia ternata _is an East Asian plant with no Sanskrit name and no entry in Caraka, Suśruta, or the nighaṇṭus. The reading below is extension on dravyaguna axes, in the register of the other reasoned-only East Asian entries (`fuling.typ`, `chrysanthemum.typ`). What is *not* reasoned, and is worth setting out first, is a genuine structural parallel: Ayurveda knows acrid Araceae very well, and handles them the same way._

*The Araceae parallel — independent discovery of the same problem.* *सूरण (sūraṇa)*, elephant-foot yam (*_Amorphophallus paeoniifolius_*), is a classical Ayurvedic dravya of the same family, and the saṃhitā and nighaṇṭu literature records exactly the property that defines 半夏: it is *कण्डू-कर (kaṇḍū-kara,* itch- and irritation-producing) in the raw state, and requires *śodhana* — processing, classically by boiling, by treatment with tamarind or buttermilk, or by burial and ageing — before it is fit to eat or prescribe. *Two pharmacopoeias, on opposite sides of Asia, independently identified the same family as acrid-but-valuable and independently arrived at processing as the answer.* That convergence is substantive; it is not, however, attestation of this species, and this entry does not treat it as such.

*Reasoned rasa:* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent) — dominant and sharp.

*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry — markedly), Tīkṣṇa (तीक्ष्ण, sharp/penetrating).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Uṣṇa (उष्ण, heating), converging with the Chinese 溫.

*Reasoned vipāka:* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).

*Prabhāva:* *None classically* — no classical entry exists. If one were proposed, the candidate is not a therapeutic action at all but the drug's *dependence on saṃskāra for its very usability*: a substance whose raw and processed forms differ not in degree but in whether they can be given to a patient. Dravyaguna has the concept to describe this — *śodhana*, purificatory processing, applied in the classical literature to aconite (*vatsanābha*), to *bhallātaka* (marking nut), and to the mineral drugs — so the right rendering is that 半夏 belongs to the *śodhana-dependent* class rather than that it possesses a prabhāva. Flagged as this book's framing.

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* *Kapha-śāmaka* (the governing effect — kaṭu-uṣṇa-rūkṣa-tīkṣṇa is the anti-Kapha combination, and phlegm is the Kapha pathology par excellence); *Vāta-śāmaka* via the uṣṇa note and the downward-directing action; *Pitta-vardhaka*, and *Vāta-vardhaka in excess* through the drying rūkṣa quality — which is the dravyaguna statement of the Chinese contraindication in yin-vacuity dry cough.

*Reasoned karma:* _Kaphahara_ and _śleṣma-hara_ (phlegm-removing); _chardighna_ (antiemetic — the action for which clove is the canonical Ayurvedic drug, see `cloves.typ`, and which 半夏 would occupy at greater strength); _vāta-anulomana_ (restoring downward flow — the dravyaguna rendering of 降逆); _dīpana-pācana_ in a mild degree; _gulma-hara_ and _śotha-hara_ for the nodule-dispersing register; *tīkṣṇa-viṣa* in the raw state, i.e. classed with the processing-dependent poisons rather than with ordinary drugs.#footnote[Reasoned placement only; _Pinellia ternata_ has no classical Ayurvedic entry. सूरण (_Amorphophallus paeoniifolius_) as a classical dravya requiring _śodhana_ for its raw acridity: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu and the standard dravyaguna literature (P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_); the _śodhana_ class for vatsanābha (aconite), bhallātaka and mineral drugs is classical. The Araceae convergence is noted as independent parallel discovery, not transmission.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(no classical Ayurvedic viruddha — non-canonical substance; the classical Chinese incompatibilities and the modern cautions are the operative ones; tags: S core saṃhitā, N nighaṇṭu, F folk/regional, M modern/pharmacological)_

- *(N-equivalent, Chinese canonical)* *半夏反烏頭* — the 十八反 antagonism with aconite, tagged here because this book's tier system has no Chinese column; textual, and *contested in practice* (see the CCM section).
- *(extended) Conditional:* _doṣa-viruddha_ in Pitta excess and in dry/Vāta states; _avasthā-viruddha_ in fluid-depleted and yin-vacuous patients — the drying action is the problem in both; _mātrā-viruddha_ and _saṃskāra-viruddha_ are the governing categories for this drug, since dose and *processing* are what separate medicine from poison here.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Raw-drug toxicity — the central item.* Ingestion of unprocessed pinellia causes immediate severe *oropharyngeal burning, numbness, salivation and oedema*, with *airway compromise* possible in significant exposures; the onset is immediate rather than delayed. Documented poisonings arise from mistaken ingestion and from improperly processed material. *This is not a theoretical risk and not a dose-dependent chronic toxicity — it is an acute mucosal injury that occurs on first exposure.*
  - *Processing quality as a safety variable.* Because every internal form depends on correct processing, *inadequately processed commercial material is itself the hazard*. Source matters here more than for almost any other drug in this book.
  - *Pregnancy* — classically cautioned yet classically prescribed (above); modern safety data are insufficient; use in pregnancy belongs with a practitioner, not with self-prescription.
  - *Interactions* — no well-characterized herb-drug interactions established for the processed forms; the absence of data should not be read as the absence of risk for a drug of this potency.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Reasoned analysis, not classical._ Pinellia ternata _is absent from Avicenna's_ Qānūn _and Ibn al-Bayṭār's_ al-Jāmiʿ _— an East Asian herb outside the reach of medieval Arabic pharmacy, which received East Asian aromatics and resins by trade but not a perishable, processing-dependent tuber. As with the Ayurveda section, however, the family is not unknown to the tradition, and the parallel is worth stating before the reasoned placement._

*The Araceae parallel in Greco-Arabic pharmacy.* *لوف (lūf)* — *Arum* species — is an attested drug of the Arabic materia medica, inherited from the Greek *ἄρον (áron)* of Dioscorides, and it carries the same profile: an acrid aroid tuber, *hot and sharp*, requiring *cooking or drying* before use, applied to swellings and skin complaints and taken internally only after preparation. *A Tibb physician handed a pinellia tuber would have recognized the kind of thing it was immediately*, and would have reached for the same corrective logic. That is a structural parallel, not an attestation.

*Names:* no classical Arabic, Persian or Urdu drug-name for this species; modern transliterations only.

*Reasoned mizāj:* *Hot and Dry, 2nd–3rd degree* — a sharp, drying, acrid drug, and in the Tibb framework an acrid aroid of this kind sits with the strong *muḥallil* and *mujaffif* simples rather than with the gentle aromatics. The raw drug would be classed frankly *toxic* (*sumūm*), a category the tradition maintains for drugs requiring correction before use.

*Reasoned afʿāl (actions):* *Mujaffif* (desiccant — the governing action, the humoral rendering of 燥濕); *muḥallil* (resolvent, for phlegmatic accumulations and nodules); *munaffith / mukhrij al-balgham* (expectorant, phlegm-expelling); *māniʿ li-l-qayʾ* (vomit-arresting — the antiemetic action, for which the tradition's own register is mint, clove and quince); *mukhaddir* in the raw state (numbing — a literal description of the raw tuber's effect on the mouth).

*Reasoned istemālāt:* *amrāḍ al-balgham* (phlegmatic diseases — the humoral analogue of the damp-phlegm indication); *suʿāl* with copious phlegm; *qayʾ* (vomiting); hard swellings and nodules, topically.

*Reasoned mawāneʿ:* hot and dry (*ṣafrāwī*, *yābis*) temperaments, and any condition of dryness or fluid depletion — the same logic as the Chinese yin-vacuity contraindication.

*Reasoned muṣliḥ / badal:* *and here the reasoned answer and the Chinese one coincide exactly.* A hot, sharp, toxic drug of this class is corrected in Tibb with *ginger*, *honey*, or a mucilaginous demulcent (*kathīrāʾ*) — and Chinese practice independently fixed on *ginger* as the corrective for this very drug. Plausible class-level *badal* within the actual Tibb pharmacopoeia: *lūf* itself, or *zarāwand* for the resolvent register. *Explicitly reasoned, not textual.*

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* *none, and none possible* — the plant was unavailable in the seventh-century Ḥijāz.#footnote[لوف (_Arum_) in the Arabic materia medica, inherited from Dioscorides' ἄρον: Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_; the acrid-aroid-requiring-preparation profile is consistent across the Greek and Arabic treatments. The convergence between the reasoned Tibb corrective (ginger) and the actual Chinese corrective for pinellia is noted as independent agreement about how to handle an acrid hot drug, not as evidence of contact.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *二陳湯 (Èr Chén Tāng,* Two Matured Decoction) — 半夏 (sovereign) with 陳皮, 茯苓, 甘草, 生薑, 烏梅; *the base formula of the entire damp-phlegm category*, and the parent of a large family. See `tangerine-peel.typ`, `fuling.typ`.
- *小半夏湯 (Xiǎo Bàn Xià Tāng)* — 半夏 + 生薑 only; the minimal antiemetic, from the *金匱要略*, and simultaneously the drug and its corrective. *小半夏加茯苓湯* adds poria for vomiting with fluid retention and is the formula used for vomiting in pregnancy.
- *半夏瀉心湯 (Bàn Xià Xiè Xīn Tāng)* — for *痞* (glomus) from mixed cold and heat; the archetype of the acrid-opening-plus-bitter-descending method. See `licorice-root.typ` for the 甘草瀉心湯 relative.
- *半夏厚朴湯 (Bàn Xià Hòu Pò Tāng)* — the canonical *梅核氣* formula; see `ginger.typ`.
- *小柴胡湯 (Xiǎo Chái Hú Tāng)* — the great harmonizing formula of the *傷寒論*, in which 半夏 handles the nausea of the shaoyang pattern.
- *溫膽湯 (Wēn Dǎn Tāng)* — phlegm-heat with insomnia and timorousness; *旋覆代赭湯* — counterflow with belching and hiccup; *麥門冬湯* — where a small amount of 半夏 is used within a largely moistening formula, a deliberate and much-discussed piece of formula design.
- *六君子湯* — Four Gentlemen plus 陳皮 and 半夏; see `formulas/four-gentlemen-decoction.typ`.
- *Preparation note:* the processed forms are decocted normally; *生半夏 is not given in decoction* for internal use. Dose selection follows the form (below).

=== Ayurveda

*No classical formula* — non-canonical plant. The *structural* analogue is the *śodhana* literature: the classical procedures for rendering *sūraṇa*, *vatsanābha* (aconite) and *bhallātaka* usable, which solve the same problem by the same strategy. See the Ayurveda section.

=== Western / Greco-Roman

*No classical attestation for this species* — but the family's European register is genuine and instructive, and amounts to the same discovery made twice more.

*Dioscorides* treats *ἄρον (áron,* _Arum maculatum_, cuckoo-pint) and *δρακόντιον (drakóntion,* _Dracunculus_) in the *De Materia Medica*, and *Pliny* follows: acrid aroid tubers, applied to swellings and sores, taken internally only after *boiling or drying*, which moderates the acridity. The *same corrective principle* as the Chinese 炮製 system and the Ayurvedic *śodhana*, arrived at independently.

*The clearest European parallel is culinary-industrial:* *Portland sago* (also "Portland arrowroot"), a starch prepared in eighteenth- and nineteenth-century Dorset from the tubers of *Arum maculatum* by *repeated grating, washing and settling* — a process whose entire purpose is to separate the starch from the acrid raphide-bearing fraction. It was sold as an invalid food. *A European cottage industry and a Chinese pharmacy independently concluded that an aroid tuber is valuable once you get the needles out.*#footnote[Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ (ἄρον, δρακόντιον) — Beck translation; Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_. Portland sago / Portland arrowroot from _Arum maculatum_ in Dorset: documented in English economic-botany and local-history literature of the 18th–19th c. The convergence of Greek, Arabic, Indian, European and Chinese practice on processing as the answer to aroid acridity is noted as independent parallel discovery.]

=== Tibb (Arabic-Islamic)

*No classical preparation for this species* — see the Tibb section above, including the *lūf* parallel.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

_Brief by necessity and by honesty: 半夏 is a toxic medicinal taken in small processed doses by indication. It is not a food, it has no dietary register in any climate, and the constitutional framework of `climates.typ` applies to it only through the pattern it treats, not through any dietary custom._

=== Warm-temperate and subtropical East Asia (the production zone)

Köppen Cfa/Cwa — Sichuan, Hubei, Gansu, Shandong and across eastern China, with Japan and Korea. *The plant is a weed of cultivated fields* — the old name *守田*, "field-guardian," records exactly that — and grows in field margins, damp woodland edges and disturbed ground rather than in cultivation proper, though demand has driven it into managed production. Tubers are lifted in summer and autumn.

*The climatic logic of its indication is real, if indirect.* Damp-phlegm patterns are the characteristic pathology of warm-humid East Asia (external 濕, Spleen-loading, per the 六淫 correspondences in `climates.typ`), and a damp-drying, phlegm-transforming drug is what that climate's pharmacopoeia needed — the same "the flora of the damp zone supplies the damp-draining drugs" pattern already noted for poria and Job's tears. The drug is used wherever the pattern presents, however, and carries no climate-specific dietary caution.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_ / _kāla-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26)

The transposition problem for this drug is not geographic but one of *supply chain and specification*. Two points. First, *processing integrity*: pinellia's safety depends entirely on correct preparation, and a globalized supplement market in which material crosses borders without pharmacopoeial oversight is precisely where inadequately processed product appears — a *saṃskāra-viruddha* in the literal sense, the wrong processing being the error. Second, *specification*: outside China the form is frequently unstated on labels and in translated formula literature, so a practitioner may not know whether they have 清半夏, 姜半夏 or 法半夏 — three drugs with different emphases. Neither problem existed when the drug was prepared by the pharmacy that dispensed it.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Not a food; no nutritional profile applies. The pharmacology of interest is the toxicology, which is unusually well characterized for a traditional drug, and a modest antiemetic literature.

*Key constituents:*
- *Calcium oxalate raphides* — bundles of *needle-shaped crystals* in specialized ejector cells (idioblasts). *These are the principal irritant principle*, and the mechanism is substantially *mechanical*: on chewing, the needles are expelled into the mucosa, producing immediate micro-lacerations, intense burning and swelling. The classical description of the raw drug's effect matches the mechanism precisely.
- *A protein/lectin component* — evidence indicates the raphides alone do not account for the whole effect, and that proteinaceous constituents carried on or with the crystals contribute to the inflammatory response. *The relative contribution is not fully settled* and should be read as an active question rather than a closed one.
- *Alkaloids and amino acids* — ephedrine-like and other minor alkaloids reported; *pinellic acid*; *homogentisic acid* and related phenolics have been proposed as irritant contributors in aroids generally.
- *Polysaccharides* and sterols.

*How processing works — the chemistry behind the 炮製.* Alum, lime, ginger juice and prolonged soaking all act to *reduce raphide content and denature the protein fraction*: soaking leaches, alkali (lime) and alum alter crystal and protein behaviour, and heating denatures. *Modern analysis confirms that correctly processed pinellia has markedly reduced irritant content*, which is a clean vindication of a procedure the tradition arrived at empirically more than a millennium before the mechanism was describable.

*Clinical and preclinical:*
- *Antiemetic* — the best-supported action, and consistent with the classical one. Pinellia-containing formulas (小半夏湯 and the Kampo preparations, including *六君子湯 / Rikkunshitō*, see `formulas/four-gentlemen-decoction.typ`) have trial support in chemotherapy-induced and functional nausea, with proposed mechanisms including 5-HT₃ and central antiemetic pathways. *Formula-level rather than single-drug evidence*, and the standard caveat applies: these trials test compounds, not pinellia alone.
- *Expectorant and antitussive* — preclinical support; consistent with 化痰.
- *Sedative, antiepileptic, anti-inflammatory, antitumour* — a scattered preclinical literature; preliminary, not clinically established.
- *What has not been shown:* that pinellia alone outperforms its formula contexts; and any robust human efficacy data for the single drug, which is unsurprising given that it is almost never prescribed alone.

*Safe dosage / upper limit — the limiting factor here is the drug itself, not a constituent:*
- *Processed forms in decoction:* *3–9 g/day* (清半夏, 姜半夏, 法半夏), with the *form selected by indication*: 姜半夏 for vomiting, 清半夏 for phlegm-damp, 法半夏 for gentler damp-drying, 竹瀝半夏 for hot phlegm.
- *生半夏 (raw):* *not for internal use* outside specialist control; external application only. *Accidental ingestion of raw tuber is a medical emergency if the airway is involved.*
- *Never self-prescribe this drug, and never substitute an unspecified "pinellia" product for a named processed form.* Of everything in this book, 半夏 is among the few substances where that instruction is not boilerplate.
- *Contraindicated:* yin-vacuity dry cough and fluid-depleted states; bleeding patterns; canonical incompatibility with aconite (contested — see above); pregnancy except under practitioner direction.
- *Source quality is a safety matter*: insist on pharmacopoeial-grade, correctly processed material from a regulated supply chain.
- *Reference thresholds, not personalized medical advice. This is a 下品 drug by the tradition's own classification — potent, toxic, and time-limited — and it belongs in the hands of a qualified practitioner.*#footnote[Calcium oxalate raphides as the principal irritant of _Pinellia ternata_, the contributory protein/lectin fraction, and the reduction of irritant content by alum, lime, ginger and soaking processing: modern Chinese and international phytochemical and toxicological literature on pinellia processing — verify against current reviews; the relative contribution of crystal and protein components is an open question and is flagged as such. Antiemetic evidence is largely formula-level (小半夏湯; Kampo 六君子湯/Rikkunshitō) — see the note on formula-level versus single-drug evidence in `formulas/four-gentlemen-decoction.typ`. Doses: _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ (current edition) monographs for 清半夏, 姜半夏, 法半夏; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

